  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A weak, slow moving cold front will continue to push \nacross the area this afternoon bringing some additional rounds of \nshowers. The rain is not expected to be as heavy as what occurred \nearlier this morning and will taper off west to east through the \nlate afternoon into this evening. \n\nAs of early this afternoon, a weak area of low pressure was centered \nnear southeastern NY State with a diffuse cold front extending \nsouthwestward along coastal NJ. Another area of low pressure \nassociated with a stronger cold front to our west was centered near \nthe lower Great Lakes. As we go through this afternoon, the initial \nfront will continue to slowly move east and southeastward pushing \noff the coast as we get into this evening. As this is occuring, we \nwill continue to get a couple more rounds of showers moving through \nthis afternoon with the focus of this activity being mainly near and \nespecially S/E of the urban corridor heading towards the coast. In \nfact north/west portions of the CWA from Berks County northward into \nthe Lehigh Valley and the southern Poconos will be drying out and \ncould even see some sunshine. Otherwise, in terms of the additional \nrain areas farther south/east will get this afternoon, it will not \nbe as heavy as what occurred earlier this morning as the greatest \ndeep layer moisture and instability are getting shunted off the\ncoast. Additional amounts of a few tenths of an inch will be \npossible but rainfall rates and amounts are not expected to be \nenough to cause any additional flooding. Still can't rule out \nthough some embedded thunderstorms, especially towards the \ncoast. Some showers could linger into the first part of the \nevening near the coast but it will otherwise be drying out with \nall precip expected to be over across the area by late evening. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The weekend will begin with drier and cooler with \nbreezy conditions for Saturday in the wake of a departing low \npressure system. Winds will diminish with temperatures moderating \nsome for Sunday. \n\nFollowing the passage of an initial weak front this evening a \nstronger secondary cold front will move through overnight as low \npressure departs to the east. In the upper levels, a closed low will \nbe slowly approaching as it moves into western PA by Saturday \nmorning. For the day Saturday, this closed low will move eastward \nright through our forecast area by later in the day. Meanwhile, low \npressure at the surface will deepen as it pulls east of New England. \nThe gradient between this low and high pressure towards the center \nof the country will result in a breezy day in addition to cooler \ntemperatures compared to our recent warmth. Generally expect highs \nfor Saturday in the upper 50s to around 60 with it feeling even \ncooler with the breeze. Expect west to WNW winds around 10-15 \ngusting up to 25 mph. The day should overall feature a mix of sun \nand clouds with a few showers possible across the southern Poconos \ndue to the upper low moving through and the tail end of some lake \neffect to the north and west. \n\nHeading into the second half of the weekend, the upper level low \ndeparts to the north and east as shortwave ridging moves in. The \npressure gradient also relaxes some as the surface low pull farther \naway into Atlantic Canada. This will result in a lighter breeze with \nslightly warmer temperatures compared to Saturday and a mainly clear \nto partly cloudy sky. Expect highs Sunday mainly in the low 60s \nfollowing morning lows mainly ranging from the upper 30s to mid 40s. \n\nGenerally dry and fair weather looks to dominate for early through \nmid next week.",
